<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Trimester, I Am Learning About...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a personal challenge and resolution. Interactive: Engage in discussions about academic and future professional interests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a personal academic challenge and resolution (e.g., "I needed help with math, so I asked my teacher."). Can engage in discussions about academic interests by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "What do you like to study?" "I like science."). Can ask and answer questions about personal learning goals to express preferences and improvement areas (e.g., "What would you like to focus on improving next trimester?" "I would like to get better at writing reports.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Two Truths and a Lie" activity related to "This Trimester, I Am Learning About..." and academic goals. Students think of three sentences, two true and one false, about their studies, career goals, or learning challenges (e.g., "I am actively learning English this trimester." "I would like to study computer science at university." "I am interested in learning history well."). Students mingle and tell their three sentences to three different classmates, who guess the lie. This activates prior vocabulary and prompts spontaneous speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher first models their own "Two Truths and a Lie," clearly articulating sentences using present continuous ("I am learning..."), gerunds ("I am interested in developing..."), and modals ("I would like to achieve..."). The teacher emphasizes stress patterns in these forms. For example, "I **am** learning English," "I **would like** to study math."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the activity and target language.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are you writing your sentences or speaking them?" (Expected answer: Speaking.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do your classmates guess which sentence is false or true?" (Expected answer: False.)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher gives an example: "If I say, 'I am learning to fly a plane,' is that true or false for me?" (Students would say false, confirming understanding of the "lie" concept.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two 8th-grade classmates discussing their academic progress and future interests. The register is informal, conversational. The format is a script for a peer-to-peer discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in discussions about personal and learning goals and offer relevant suggestions for improvement, respecting others opinions or thoughts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the dialogue, explaining it's about two friends talking about school.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud once for gist, modeling natural pace and intonation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher rereads, pausing to highlight and explain new vocabulary (e.g., "trimester," "career goals," "scholarship," "motivated," "curious," "achieve," "develop skills") and target grammar (present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, modals for desires) in context. Teacher writes key phrases on the board.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and repeat key phrases with the teacher, focusing on stress patterns in "I am learning" and "I would like."</li>
                </ol>
                <b>Example Dialogue Snippet:</b><br>
                <b>Maria:</b> "Hi, Carlos! How are you doing in science this trimester? I heard you're actively learning a lot."<br>
                <b>Carlos:</b> "Hey Maria! I'm doing well, thanks! I am really enjoying the experiments. I'm interested in developing my knowledge about space. What about you? What subjects are you studying?"<br>
                <b>Maria:</b> "I am focusing on history and English. History is challenging, but I'm trying to improve. I would like to achieve good grades this trimester. I'm also curious about computer science for my career goals."<br>
                <b>Carlos:</b> "That's great! You should keep practicing to get better at history. I'm also thinking about university and what skills I need."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What subject is Carlos enjoying?" (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: Maria is interested in developing her computer science skills for her career goals." (True/False)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice speaking using present continuous for ongoing actions, gerunds after prepositions, modals for ability and desires, and the target vocabulary (education, skills, knowledge, career goals, subject, course, university, scholarship, trimester, history, classmate, schedule, learn, study, develop, grow, achieve, educational, critical, academic, motivated, ambitious, curious, actively, well). Pronunciation practice on stress patterns and minimal pairs /ɪ/ vs /iː/ (skill vs skull) and /æ/ vs /e/ (had vs head).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder (Oral):</b> Teacher provides sentence frames on the board, and students complete them orally, focusing on correct grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This trimester, I am actively _______ (verb+ing) _______ (subject)." (e.g., "I am actively learning English.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am interested in _______ (verb+ing) my _______ (noun)." (e.g., "I am interested in developing my skills.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I would like to _______ (verb) _______ (goal)." (e.g., "I would like to achieve a scholarship.")</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher provides immediate feedback on pronunciation, especially stress patterns in modals and continuous forms, and corrects any usage errors. The teacher also conducts quick, focused oral drills on minimal pairs like "skill" and "skull" or "had" and "head" by saying words and having students identify the sound or repeat after the teacher for clear production.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Interview Grid:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a grid with prompts like: "What subject are you actively learning well this trimester?", "What skill are you interested in developing?", "What career goal would you like to achieve?" They take turns asking and answering, using the target language. The teacher monitors closely, listening for correct use of grammar and pronunciation, and providing assistance as needed. Pragmatic focus: asking about progress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Sentence Check:</b> Students form new pairs. They share one sentence from the previous activity with their new partner, who listens and checks for correct grammar and clear pronunciation. The listener then rephrases their partner's sentence or asks a follow-up question. (e.g., "So, you're saying you're interested in learning more about history?"). Sociolinguistic focus: respecting others' opinions and active listening.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play: "Peer Academic Counseling." Students will work in pairs to simulate a conversation where one student seeks advice or shares a challenge, and the other offers encouragement and suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into pairs. One student is "Student A" (seeking advice/sharing a challenge), and the other is "Student B" (offering advice/suggestions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A:</b> Describes a personal academic challenge from "this trimester" (e.g., "I am finding it hard to actively learn math," or "I am struggling with writing reports, I need to develop my skills.") and expresses a learning goal (e.g., "I would like to get better at writing reports to achieve higher grades.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B:</b> Listens attentively, asks follow-up questions about progress (e.g., "How are you doing in math?"), and offers relevant suggestions for improvement and encouragement using the target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "You should keep practicing. Maybe you are interested in joining a study group to grow your knowledge?" or "You could ask your teacher for extra help. I believe you can achieve your goals if you are motivated and curious.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles after 3-4 minutes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors, listening for fluency, collaborative interaction, and appropriate use of target language, taking notes for later feedback without interrupting.</li>
                </ul>
                This task directly connects to the theme "This Trimester, I Am Learning About..." and the scenario "Education and Learning" by having students discuss real or imagined academic situations. It requires students to describe challenges and resolutions (productive speaking) and engage in discussions about academic interests, asking and responding to questions, and offering suggestions (interactive speaking), fulfilling the specific objective and learning outcome.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a simple checklist for each pair's performance during the "Peer Academic Counseling" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Peer Academic Counseling" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear stress patterns in modals and continuous forms; sounds of /ɪ/ and /iː/, /æ/ and /e/ are generally distinct.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs at least 5-7 words from the list (e.g., skills, knowledge, career goals, subject, trimester, learn, study, develop, grow, achieve, motivated, curious).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Uses present continuous (e.g., "I am learning"), gerunds after prepositions (e.g., "interested in developing"), and modals (e.g., "I would like to study," "You should keep practicing").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Ideas are expressed clearly; contributions are relevant to the discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Actively listens, asks and responds to questions appropriately, offers encouragement and suggestions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket before leaving class.
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used effectively today when talking about your studies?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel describing a learning challenge and suggesting a resolution in English (1-5, 5 being very confident)?"</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written):</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities did you find most helpful for improving your English communication?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one area you would like to focus on improving in your speaking next time?"</li>
                </ol></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The role-play encouraged authentic use of the target language and allowed students to engage in discussions about personal and learning goals naturally. The integration of pronunciation practice for stress patterns seemed effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still struggle with spontaneously forming sentences with gerunds after prepositions; perhaps more targeted sentence builder activities could be beneficial. I need to ensure all students are actively participating in the pair work and not just listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: The vocabulary and grammar used in discussing academic challenges and goals will be essential for the upcoming writing lesson (Lesson 4), where students will compose short paragraphs about their learning experiences and aspirations. We will build on these communicative functions to structure written ideas effectively.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>